<LearningGuideLayout
  title="How to Make Doors and Switches Work from Blender"
  description="Create an animated door or switch in Blender and connect it to FPE triggers, Scene Events, audio, and Godot gameplay."
  summary="A good interactive door is tiny theater: the player approaches, something notices, motion begins, sound lands on the right beat, and the world changes. Blender already contains the stage, prop, timing, and blocking—so author the mechanic there too."
  relatedSlugs={[
    "create-triggers-in-blender-for-godot",
    "animation-frame-events-godot",
    "play-3d-audio-godot",
  ]}
  fpeHeading="What FPE creates in Godot"
>
  <div class="guide-summary">
    <strong>You will make:</strong> an animated Blender door driven by a nearby trigger
    or switch, with an optional sound placed on the exact animation frame.
  </div>
  <GuideFigure
    src="/assets/images/guides/blender/door-action-timeline.png"
    alt="Workshop door action in Blender with closed and open rotation keyframes"
    caption="Animate the physical door and its collision together. This example moves from the closed pose at frame 1 to the open pose at frame 48."
  />
  <h2>Animate the prop first in Blender</h2><ol>
    <li>Give the door and its moving part clear names.</li><li>
      Set the hinge or sliding origin correctly before animating.
    </li><li>
      Create an action such as <code>Door_Open</code> and animate only the transform
      needed for the motion. If Blender animation is new to you, begin with Blender's
      <a
        href="https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/animation/keyframes/introduction.html"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">keyframe introduction</a
      >, then see <a
        href="https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/animation/actions.html"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">Actions</a
      > and the <a
        href="https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/latest/editors/dope_sheet/modes/action.html"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">Action Editor</a
      >.
    </li><li>
      Preview the action in Blender and check that it does not clip through
      surrounding art.
    </li><li>
      In <strong>Folded Paper Engine (Animation)</strong>, leave <strong
        >Autoplay</strong
      > off. Enable <strong>Loop</strong> only when repetition is genuinely intended.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <GuideFigure
    src="/assets/images/guides/blender/trigger-events-panel.png"
    alt="Door trigger configured to dispatch OPEN_WORKSHOP_DOOR from Blender"
    caption="The nearby interaction volume dispatches a readable event instead of knowing how the door animation is implemented."
  />
  <h2>Add the activation</h2><p>
    For an automatic door, place a trigger volume across the approach and
    dispatch a semantic event such as <code>OPEN_WORKSHOP_DOOR</code>. For a
    deliberate switch, author the switch object and use a trigger or custom
    interaction to dispatch that same event. Keeping one event name means either
    input can operate the door without duplicating the result.
  </p>
  <GuideFigure
    src="/assets/images/guides/blender/scene-events-panel.png"
    alt="FPE Scene Events panel with OPEN_WORKSHOP_DOOR configured"
    caption="Scene Events receives the trigger name and assigns the scene action that should run."
  />
  <h2>Build the Scene Event</h2><ol>
    <li>
      Create the matching event in Blender's <strong>Scene Events</strong> panel.
    </li><li>Add the door action to <strong>Animations</strong>.</li><li>
      If the door has a positioned sound, mark its source object as a <strong
        >Speaker</strong
      > and trigger it from the same event.
    </li><li>
      If the sound must land on a precise latch or impact frame, use a Frame
      Event instead of starting it at the beginning.
    </li><li>
      For a one-way transition, add the level-loading command only after the
      visual timing is proven.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <h2>Make it robust</h2><ul>
    <li>Prevent the same opening animation from stacking repeatedly.</li><li>
      Use separate open and close events when the motion must reverse cleanly.
    </li><li>
      Keep the invisible trigger slightly away from moving geometry.
    </li><li>
      Test with a second participant or physics object to confirm filtering.
    </li><li>
      Do not hide unique puzzle rules inside the animation; dispatch a named
      event that Godot code can extend.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Fragment slot="fpe"
    ><p>
      FPE imports the action, trigger, speaker, and Scene Event commands as
      cooperating Godot features. The result remains inspectable: an engineer
      can add locks, save-state checks, prompts, or bespoke puzzle logic while
      the artist retains the door's placement, animation, sound timing, and
      activation space in Blender.
    </p><p>
      <a href="/docs/fpe-triggers-commands.html"
        >Read the trigger and command catalog</a
      > and <a href="/docs/fpe-animation-and-events.html"
        >the animation event reference</a
      >.
    </p></Fragment
  >
</LearningGuideLayout>
